  "enhanced_information": "The patient presents with symptoms and laboratory findings consistent with Cushing's syndrome, specifically ACTH-dependent Cushing's syndrome, as indicated by elevated serum ACTH levels. The next step is to differentiate between a pituitary source (Cushing's disease) and an ectopic source of ACTH production. The high-dose dexamethasone suppression test is a standard approach to differentiate between these sources. In Cushing's disease, cortisol production is typically suppressed by high-dose dexamethasone, whereas ectopic ACTH production is not. Therefore, the most appropriate next step in evaluation is the high-dose dexamethasone suppression test. The retrieved evidence paths, while broad, confirm the association of corticotropin (ACTH) with Cushing's syndrome, supporting the need to further investigate the source of ACTH production.",
